Description: Given a string s consisting of lowercase English letters and a positive integer k, return the total number of substrings that contain exactly k distinct characters.
A substring is a contiguous sequence of characters within a string.

Example 1:

Input: s = "pqpqs", k = 2

Output: 7

Explanation: All substrings with exactly 2 distinct characters are: "pq", "pqp", "pqpq", "qp", "qpq", "pqs", "qs".
The total count is 7.

Example 2:

Input: s = "abcbaa", k = 3

Output: 5

Explanation: All substrings with exactly 3 distinct characters are: "abc", "abcb", "abcba", "bcba", "cbaa". 
The total count is 5.

1. Brute Force:
The most naive way to solve this is generate every possible substring count the number of distinct chracters in each and check if it exactly equals k 

int countSubstrings(string s, int k) {
    int n = s.length();
    int count = 0;

    //Generate all starting point
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        //Generate all ending points 
        for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
            unordered_set<char> distinctChars;
            //Check distinct chracters in the current substring
            for (int x = i; x <= j; x++) {
                distinctChars.insert(s[x]);
            }
            if (distinctChars.size() == k) {
                count++;
            }
        }
    }
    return count;
}

TC: O(n^3) 
n is length of string 
nested loop takes O(n^2) to generate start and end points and inserting it into the set 
takes O(n) in worst case.

SC: O(k)
for storing upto k distinct chracters in the ordered_set

2. Better Approach
We can optimize the brute force by avoiding the third loop. 
Instead of re-evaluating the entire substring from scratch, we can maintain the character frequencies on the fly as we expand our right pointer j. 
Once the distinct character count exceeds $k$, we can immediately break out of the inner loop since adding more characters will never decrease the distinct count.

int countSubstrings(string s, int k) {
    int n = s.length();
    int count = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        vector<int> freq(26, 0);
        int distinct = 0;

        for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
            if (freq[s[j] - 'a'] == 0) { //here freq is 0
                distinct++; //so distinct increases and increments the freq
            }

            if (distinct == k) {
                count++;
            } else if (distinct > k) {
                //No need to check further if we already exceeded the k distinct characters 
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    return count;
}

TC: O(n^2) 
n is length of string
2 nested loops traverse the string 
Inner operation takes O(1) time using a fixed size frequency 

SC: O(1)
fixed size array of 26

3. Optimal Approach 
To achieve linear time complexity we can use sliding window(two-pointer) technique.

Directly finding substrings with exactly k distinct chracters using a sliding window is tricky.
Coz shrinking the window when you have exactly k chracters might cause you to miss valid 
substrings. Instead we use a math trick:
Exactly(k) = Atmost(k) - AtMost(k - 1)

We write a helper func atMost(s, k) that finds the number of strings with at most k distinct chracters in O(n) time 

int atMostKDistinct(const string& s, int k) {
    if (k < 0) return 0;

    vector<int> freq(26, 0);
    int left = 0; int right = 0;
    int distinct = 0;
    int totalSubstrings = 0;

    while (right < s.length()) {
        //Expand the window by adding the right character 
        if (freq[s[right] - 'a'] == 0) {
            distinct++;
        }
        freq[s[right] - 'a']++;

        //Shrink the window from the left if distinct characters exceed k 
        while (distinct > k) {
            freq[s[left] - 'a']--;
            if (freq[s[left] - 'a'] == 0) {
                distinct--;
            }
            left++;
        }

        //Number of valid substrings ending at 'right' is (right - left + 1)
        totalSubstrings += (right - left + 1);
        right++;
    }

    return totalSubstrings;
}

int countSubstrings(string s, int k) {
    return atMostKDistinct(s, k) - atMostKDistinct(s, k - 1);
}

TC: O(n)
n is the length of string 
both left and right pointers traverse the string from left to right exactly once in the atMostKDistinct func
Calling it twice takes O(2n) = O(n) time 

SC: O(1)

